Shared virtual shadow maps
==========================

Source proposal: [Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Proposal.md](Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Proposal.md)
Status: Phases 1–4 retain receipts for their original contracts. Phase 5's committed draft checkpoint
has been refactored and qualified within Phase 7's controlled foundation scope. Phase 6's history
investigation and support assessment and Phase 7 are complete. Phase 8A's native geometry and Phase 8B's
fixed-capacity texture-array migration are qualified within their controlled scope; Phase 8 is complete.
Shadows remain disabled. Phase 9 and all later implementation work remain open.
The baseline uses the system's established OpenGL 4.5 plus
GL_ARB_gl_spirv requirement; shader ingestion is not a new VSM platform decision.
Authority: the user's approved atomic rendering and bounded temporal reuse decisions below
supersede conflicting portions of the earlier proposal and plan. Unaffected proposal requirements remain
binding. Phase 7 reconciles the proposal, implementation contracts and qualification protocol before
dependent code changes. Numerical starting values remain tuning hypotheses, not measured guarantees.

Goal and governing contracts
  Deliver one bounded virtual shadow system for simultaneous moving sun/moon shadows and local
  point lights, with carried and moving lights treated as primary performance workloads. Complete
  opaque/cutout and transparent surface shadows before fully implementing shadowed volumetric fog.
  Use the existing chunk grid and native dimension-aware identity. GPU compute generates conservative
  chunk occlusion candidates from fixed partition boxes over covered light motion. Candidate reuse
  and rendered-depth reuse have separate validity rules. Do not omit required occluders or publish
  truncated work as complete. Permit explicitly bounded temporal reuse of previously complete depths;
  reduce detail and then withdraw lights' shadows when neither current nor permitted stale coverage fits.
  Preserve native poolsByRenderPass[renderPass][atlas] authority and existing geometry ownership.
  No parallel mesh-pool registry, reverse classification map, per-page pool scan, copied terrain
  meshes, duplicate voxel connectivity analysis, or collection of pool-membership hooks.
  OpenGL 4.5 plus GL_ARB_gl_spirv is the established system baseline (core SPIR-V support also satisfies
  ingestion). Use compact draw/view identifiers, separate projection records,
  virtual-coordinate rasterization, page-table translation and atomic integer texture depth writes.
  Keep bounded initialized indirect intervals without requiring optional shader draw parameters,
  GPU draw-count buffers, bindless access or positive vertex/TES/geometry-stage SSBO limits.
  The approved Phase 6B direction derives clip-distance usage from compiled SPIR-V and packages it in
  existing per-stage shader metadata. Production shaders remain precompiled SPIR-V; no new runtime GLSL
  compilation or parallel metadata system. Phase 7 implements and qualifies this support through
  controlled fixtures; Phase 8 integrates it with actual native shadow rendering.
  Keep surface demand and readiness ahead of every participating surface consumer. Include all
  contributing transparent layers, filter footprints and receiver-position bias. Previous-frame
  feedback may prioritize work but cannot certify current coverage. One light/consumer has one
  shadow provider; no duplicate native/VSM attenuation or hidden native coarse fallback.
  Record each depth page's receiving coverage, including depth, separately from page identity.
  New receivers and fog samples outside that coverage require complete replacement depths.
  Publish non-terrain caster changes, including entry, disappearance and deformation, before reuse.
  Use the loaded-chunk envelope per dimension, including offscreen partitions. Interior unknown
  cells are unresolved, not empty. Do not silently introduce an additional caster-distance cap.
  Outside-envelope terrain is an explicit client world-data limitation, not guaranteed shadow coverage.

Approved atomic rendering architecture
  Use the approved ordinary-mesh virtual shadow architecture: GPU-selected geometry/view work,
  compact identifiers into separate projection records, virtual-coordinate rasterization, per-fragment
  physical-page lookup and atomic nearest-depth accumulation. This replaces per-page geometry instances,
  geometry-stage layer routing, full-matrix vertex attributes and layered depth-attachment clearing.
  Preserve the game's existing geometry ownership and scene-publication boundaries.

  The physical pool is resident integer image storage with tiles addressed through the page table.
  Phase 7 fixes its atlas/array organization, finite depth encoding, clear value, comparison direction
  and resource limits together. R32UI atomic min/max with a documented ordered depth encoding is the
  baseline; do not assume floating-point atomics or preserve D32F attachment semantics.
  Renderability differs from residency and sampling eligibility. A shader may update only an admitted
  replacement tile belonging to the correct view/assignment. Cached or retained stale tiles must not
  be modified merely because they are resident. Atomics do not certify page completeness, replace
  material cutout rejection, or remove resource ordering before sampling.

  The architecture does not establish measured VGE performance or complete OpenGL compatibility.
  Separate static/dynamic caches and alternative geometry rasterizers are outside the required scope.

Approved temporal reuse amendment
  Decision: bounded reuse of previously complete shadow depths is required from the first integrated
  surface implementation, not an optional later optimization. Temporarily missing or lingering shadows
  after scene changes are an accepted approximation within the configured policy. This does not certify
  those depths as current, permit partial replacement pages, or authorize obsolete native geometry references.

  Keep three distinct states: current, permitted stale, and unavailable. Scene edits, retessellation,
  caster motion and light motion invalidate currentness but need not immediately discard a retained depth
  image. Eligibility for stale use is a separate bounded decision, not an exception to revision checking.
  Stable projection-space addressing is independent of mesh allocation: resolve every replacement draw
  through the chunk's current native ranges; retain depth storage and its own metadata for stale sampling.
  No per-vertex page ownership, native mesh-pool mutation or reverse pool registry is introduced.

  Preserve the rendered projection, depth convention, receiving coverage, source lifetime and physical
  assignment with each retained result. Sample stale depth using its matching rendered projection, never
  by interpreting it through a newer light matrix. Keep direct illumination on the current light snapshot;
  any lag in shadow position is an explicit approximation. Cover current receiver/filter/bias requests
  in that retained projection. New receivers outside its recorded coverage cannot use it, and current
  candidate associations cannot enlarge old depth coverage. Cube faces, levels and filter taps must use
  compatible projection metadata; do not combine incompatible old/new projections in one lookup.

  Persist finite stale-age and light-motion tolerances with validated bounds, requested/effective values
  and a strict zero-reuse setting for diagnosis. Set concrete defaults and the translation/angular error
  metric in Phase 7, then calibrate in Phase 11; no arbitrary value is approved by this amendment.
  Record rendered age and the first invalidation time. Repeated edits, motion, scheduler deferral or reuse
  cannot restart the stale deadline. Candidate motion coverage is not a stale-depth motion tolerance.
  Apply the policy to moving/carried point lights and both celestial sources from the outset.

  Lifetime/identity loss, reassigned or destroyed storage, incompatible resource/shader interpretation,
  out-of-coverage receivers, expired age or exceeded motion tolerance prohibit reuse. Incomplete or
  unresolved current inputs prohibit fresh certification; they do not by themselves erase an otherwise
  eligible retained result. Unknown temporal eligibility fails closed. One light/consumer may remain
  shadowed only when all of its current demand has current or permitted stale coverage; do not expose
  page-shaped missing coverage. No stale result means the existing unshadowed-light fallback applies.

  Retain old complete depth while replacement work is pending. Never clear or overwrite storage still
  serving that result: use bounded replacement storage and switch publication only after complete draws,
  or explicitly withdraw the result before destructive reuse. Charge retained/replacement depths,
  projection records and in-flight retirement to the existing configurable budgets. Memory pressure can
  force withdrawal; it cannot authorize over-budget retention. Preserve stable priorities and hysteresis,
  with bounded refresh waiting so continuous edits or motion cannot indefinitely postpone updates.

  Implementation sequencing: Phase 5 checkpoints the current draft; Phase 6 investigates clipping history
  and support requirements; Phase 7 refactors foundations and adds temporal storage/sampling.
  Phase 8 completes native atomic rendering. Phase 9 integrates
  scheduling/readiness, Phase 10 migrates surface consumers, Phase 11 qualifies them, Phase 12 implements
  fog last, and Phase 13 closes qualification and cleanup. Earlier receipts retain their original scope;
  they do not establish acceptance of the replacement architecture.

Execution and evidence rules
  Complete each related implementation batch before its full verification and review cycle; use an
  intermediate check only for a concrete uncertainty blocking the next decision. Keep all unchecked
  items open until their stated evidence exists. Reconcile affected permanent documentation and
  linked proposal requirements before completing a phase. Perform a second review and independent
  completion audit on the finished phase; batch confirmed corrections before re-verification.
  Use subagents for every build/test invocation. Before writing or reviewing tests, load the active
  test-workflow skill and use its configured specialists. Use canonical repository build/test and
  package routes discovered in Phase 1; do not invent alternate success criteria or invoke them now.
  Distinguish source/API inspection, deterministic unit checks, real GPU integration, engine lifecycle
  integration, package verification, measured performance, and user-run visual acceptance. A headless
  pass proves only its exercised boundary. Never launch the game for visual acceptance; retain the
  user's observations and attributable runtime receipts. Missing live evidence leaves that gate open.
  Follow repository method/class documentation, visibility regions and steady-state source layout.
  Keep composition roots thin, geometry ownership native and dependencies directional. Do not add
  temporary forwarding facades or production compatibility shims to bridge unfinished phases.
  Phase 5 is an explicit checkpoint exception: completion means the existing draft is committed with
  truthful inventory/status, not that the former feature contract passed. Do not expand that checkpoint
  into implementation repair or a feature-completion test/audit cycle. Phase 6 is analysis only: review
  its evidence, reasoning and affected-system inventory without requiring runtime implementation proof.
  Phases 7 onward retain the full implementation verification, second-review and independent
  completion-audit gates.
  Phases 2–4 and 7–9 qualify foundations through fixtures and controlled integration; production consumers
  switch coherently in Phase 10. This is not permission to ship a partially covered shadow path.

Assumptions, tuning hypotheses and decision gates
  Concrete API names, shader layouts, quality-profile ceilings and safe native publication seams
  are implementation decisions, not reasons to reopen established requirements. Record their
  resolution in the [implementation contracts](Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Contracts.md) and
  [qualification protocol](Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Qualification.md), distinguishing design from runtime.
  The proposal's inspected engine is Vintage Story 1.22.7; verify the actual supported assembly and
  relevant contracts before using that receipt as current evidence. Do not assume ChunkDirty covers
  every load, unload, upload and retirement event, or that cached Empty/connectivity is always fresh.
  Connectivity pruning is an optional proven optimization. Bounds-only selection remains the required
  conservative reference/fallback wherever freshness or shadow-opacity equivalence is unproven.
  Hardware-specific CPU/GPU limits, minimum useful shadow-ready duty cycle and visual quality targets
  need an explicit acceptance profile. Establish the measurement protocol in Phase 1 and settle
  measured values in Phase 11; do not treat the tentative 2 ms target as an achieved hard ceiling.
  Point near-plane, bias, depth range and extreme directional spans require qualification. Existing
  represented depth-clamp support may be evaluated. Phase 6 re-evaluates the custom-clipping exclusion;
  analysis alone does not enable support or change the runtime contract.

  Proposed initial values from the source, to be validated and calibrated rather than hard-coded:
  | Control | Initial hypothesis |
  | --- | --- |
  | Total shadow resources | 256 MiB, including depth, metadata, associations, commands and temporary generations |
  | GPU scheduling target | 2 ms/frame for all shadow work, including demand preparation |
  | Simultaneous shadowed local lights | 8; separate from the existing illumination-list capacity |
  | Directional reservation | 50% of memory and scheduled work; locals reserve the remainder; unused shares borrowable |
  | Surface quality | Balanced profile with bounded refinement and finite internal work ceilings |
  | Candidate movement coverage | Directional spherical cap: 1-degree radius; point-source box: 2-metre half extents |
  | Refresh guard | Start replacement in the outer quarter of covered angular distance or box extent |
  | Selection/recovery | 20% challenger margin; 0.5-second hold while valid; eight complete current frames for recovery |
  | Page/filter tuning | R32UI atomic depth; 128 by 128 virtual pages; no physical mip chain, gutters or multisampling; manual 3-by-3 PCF |

  Optional direct layer output, indirect counts, shader draw parameters, bindless sampling, hierarchies,
  physical-pool compaction by copying and larger filters/contact hardening are
  not required implementation work in this plan. Do not add them as substitutes for qualifying the
  baseline. Any scope change or required-feature deferral needs explicit reconciliation and approval;
  an unresolved requirement is not a completed task.

